temperature  and that's going to be the temperature  which you know I'm not  for for some operational issues normally  hydrates but the nice thing is because  it's you know it's an interpolating  function I can also look at these  results not just in the time domain but  I can also look at I can look at Matt in  the spatial domain and sort of the  radial distance in the pipeline so how  do we decide to develop Alex we had a  couple options we had Excel  visual studio we had Mathematica and you  know I'm obviously a huge supporter of  Mathematica but at the end of the day  what it really came down to was that we  needed something that we could belt very  fast like we're not we're not in the R&amp;D  business we're in the you know clients  and project business and something so we  needed needed something that worked it  was accurate and and we could build it  really quickly and so and develop 37  different utilities inside of visual  basis is not something that's gonna take  the amount of time that we needed it to  or though it doesn't mount of time that  we had so so the code structure is so  that the rest of the slides that I just  want to go through really kind of  briefly on each one how you know I've  approached a certain issue related to  application development with the Wolfram  language and and just to give you guys  an idea of how it's all set up so alex  is contained in a package that's really  nice for naming conventions it makes it  really easy to isolate your code and  using the different you know the colon  key or whatever you can isolate it and  grab a particular variable from a  certain context I also like the package  editor basically just because it allows  me to collapse these different sections  and I can organize it really nicely I  can I can have you know an image  individual module and then everything  related to that and and isolate out the  methods from the UI and things like that  and the other thing that's nice is when  you have it in a package as opposed to  see yet you can protect that and you can  encode it and and that way your  intellectual property is safe so the  insights into the UI really quick the  whole so the whole thing is obviously in  the CDF on the top we've got a docked  cell and then the remainder of the the  notebook runs is all wrapped around a  dynamic module wrapped inside of a  dynamic module and then each individual  utility  has its own dynamic module and then  inside of that is a pain because a lot  of these extend beyond on the screen and  we wanted something that we could scroll  through and then over here that  searchable field that I mentioned before  and then just a pane here with a with a  column of buttons in each one if you  click it it you know it sets the the  appropriate utility that you want to  view so all of us have written  Mathematica code we know that there's no  compiling and that's that's what's great  about it I did want to have something  that would allow me to build Aleks and  what it basically does is I brought this  function that compiles to Kyle Aleks and  it and it takes the file as a package  and it moves it over to the appropriate  user base directory for Mathematica and  for C or for the CDF player and then in  the process it encodes it so that it's  it's protected and you can't can't view  it in Roth text form and what it also  will do is update a version number that  that I can maintain separate from the  utility and then if I want I can it has  an option that it will accept its own  version number so if I want to increment  the next version number I can do that  manually  so version control it's you know it's  critical for any successful software  project and in Alex because it's in a  package form and it's a text form it's  very easy to maintain it using it and  because it's raw text you can do discs  on the different versions really easily  so it's it's nice as opposed to using a  notebook which has a lot of cells and a  different a lot of additional text if  you actually look inside of it and and  that makes it a little bit more  difficult for the version control  updates when I first started working on  our we was updates sometimes twice a  week and I always wanted to make sure  that people had an easy way to get the  latest version so I have all the files  sort of associated with Alex in one  central spot and then I wrote this  little executable here and that'll copy  those files to the the appropriate spot  on their computer system under there  under that same user user base directory  and that way when the when the Alex  launches it knows exactly where to go to  look for those for those files and it  can load them in really easily  so this is something I I've only  recently started doing actually and I  knew that Wolfram had automated testing  but I hadn't had a chance to really look  at it yet and and once I did I was I was  really impressed I mean just just like  anything else with real form it's it's  really easy to test your functions and  you can write you know a few few lines  to change some variables and account for  different things that the user might do  and then you can run a test and set that  up as a test and then when you run it  you'll easily see if you if you broken  anything with the past version stuff  like that which which I tend to do so  it's nice to have this bug reporting so  right away when I first built this I  built in the upper right corner of this  little button report bug and it uses the  the send mail function to basically send  me send me a email and with that email  I'll get information about all the  variables and that utility what current  utility they're using and it also sent  me you can take a front-end screen shot  using Mathematica code and it'll send me  a screenshot of that and lots of times I  can see you know what are the red boxes  or something you know something's not  working right but you really need some  some way to do that because Wolfram's  really good about not letting their  program crash so you don't necessarily  know that it just it just broke you just  know that something's not really working  right and so I needed some way to report  that and let people know and and now I  probably get one of these every every  two to three weeks so so there's been  improvement debugging I know there are  ways of debugging inside of the package  inside of the notebook interface and  other other areas but I tend to just do  it directly in Mathematica I like to be  able to do exactly whatever my colleague  that broke something whatever they were  doing I like to repeat those steps in  here and then I'll generally just look  at you know the exact variables and and  I can look at you know just the raw data  and things like that I can kind of  figure out what's going on like that  it's that's how I'll generally approach  it just a couple extras one of the big  things that we wanted was an easy way to  get data out of the utility so we used  to when I originally wrote this we had  an export function and what it would do  is let someone save the file somewhere  and then Excel file a CSV file and you  kind of file and then you know it  export that data and then when they  needed that they would have to go open  that data and I was always really  interested in the idea of the copy to  clipboard but if you've ever used it in  Mathematica and you and you actually  copied the data it's really nice for  pasting in between notebooks but it's  not really nice if you want to paste it  to Excel so so I wrote my own my own  data and and then when I paste that it  looks a little weird right here but if  you were to paste that in Excel to be  tab-separated and and everything would  look really nice so that's that's built  into all the utilities and you can just  export all that data really easily and  you don't have to do that that second  and third step the save load every every  utility that's in there right now has  has an option to save the current  configuration and I wanted something  that was really generic to do that and  so I basically wrote this this little  code right here and it'll take the name  whatever the utility is and then all my  variables associated with that it'll  write them as an association to a file  with the string as the variable and then  the value and then similarly when I load  that back in I can take those those same  strings and just convert them into into  symbols and then set the values and and  it works really nicely and I don't ever  have to manually go through and set  which variables I want to say it just  it's handled for each utility okay  finally  so advantages of Wolfram language for  application development algorithms can  be developed very quickly and you  generally require a lot less code which  which I'm sure all of you are aware of  but when it comes to things like R&amp;D  projects it's really nice to have and  its really nice to do it that way  limitations for me as far as what I saw  with application development there were  some strong limitations and the UI  capabilities I don't think you're ever  going to be able to develop a  professional you know looking desktop  application you know there's just some  limits in the in the way the notebook is  set up but I mean I think you can get to  something that looks pretty nice and and  especially if it's for quick you know  internal development I think it'll  probably meet any requirements that you  have  so conclusion so future Daleks we've got  a growing list of utilities to add right  now probably you know we're at 37 I  think I'll probably easily get up to 50  we're dealing a lot more with the lab  now with our lab and sort of coupling  the engineering work with the lab and  there's there's a lot of opportunities  there to expand further with that and  I've been really interested in the the  cloud opportunities and learning  specifically about API functions is way  to to call those instead of running  these things through CDF so that all  that might be in the future  so Wolfram language obviously plays a  very vital role at a fast not just for  me I use it personally and every sort of  work function I do but also in the  ability to distribute these the CDF to  my colleagues which get used daily as  well and lastly you know I think we're  all aware that Wolfram language can play  a huge role in the industry and sort of  preaching to the choir obviously but you  know I think it's all of our jobs to you  know just increase that awareness and  you know keep doing what we're doing  spreading the word because it's a really  powerful tool and that's it thank you  [Applause]
 

